// causal.jsonl — 5 atoms × 15 probes (5×L1, 5×L2, 5×L3) = 75 probes total.
// Atoms: causal.mechanism_inference, causal.intervention_prediction,
//        causal.implicit_inference, causal.counterfactual_prediction,
//        causal.hidden_state_inference.
// Scorer types used: mcq_letter (65), exact_match_string (0 after revision), regex (5),
//                    substring_all (0 after revision). All free-text answers
//                    in causal.hidden_state_inference.L3.* use anchored regex
//                    to tolerate common compound phrasings (e.g.
//                    'kitchen knife', 'duck-billed platypus', 'brasil',
//                    'marie sklodowska-curie') without leaking into wrong answers.
// Surface domains rotated within each level (medicine, appliance, botany,
// software, ecology, chemistry, geology, hvac, climate, economics, genetics,
// physics, narrative, paleobiology, hardware, probability, twenty-questions, etc.)
// to reduce domain-specific shortcut.
// All MCQ probes specify the option list inline and an explicit output
// instruction; free-text probes specify the exact answer format.
//
// Subagent review (Explore) was applied. Probes fixed include:
//   - mechanism_inference.L1.002: car/key scenario replaced with dishwasher
//     (verbatim leakage from atom card excerpt 'turns the key in the ignition').
//   - mechanism_inference.L2.005: re-domained from web-service to HVAC to remove
//     duplicate diagnostic structure with L2.002.
//   - mechanism_inference.L3.002: cosmic-ray distractor replaced with realistic
//     GC-pause regression option.
//   - intervention_prediction.L1.003: option D rewritten to remove 'increases (frees up)'
//     ambiguity.
//   - counterfactual_prediction.L1.001: Fleming/penicillin replaced with Chicxulub
//     to escape 'famous-scientist counterfactual' template echoing card excerpt.
//   - counterfactual_prediction.L3.002: tag corrected to 'coupled_feedback'.
//   - hidden_state_inference.L1.005: replaced isomorphic coin probe with
//     LED/voltage-rail probe to avoid duplication with L1.001.
//   - hidden_state_inference.L2.001: replaced d6/d10/d20 reuse with biased-urn
//     likelihood-ranking probe (Bayesian update over a 3-element hypothesis space).
//   - hidden_state_inference.L2.003: 'unsharpened pencil tip' cue replaced with
//     graphite-transfer smudges (a coherent forensic signal).
//   - hidden_state_inference.L3.001-005: scorers tightened to anchored regex,
//     accepting standard compound forms but rejecting unrelated text.
//   - implicit_inference.L2.003 and L2.004: re-domained to remove hunger and
//     bad-news/withdrawal overlap with L1.002 and L1.003 respectively.
//
// Notes on declines (issues raised by review and consciously NOT fixed):
//   - hidden_state_inference.L1.002 (apples/oranges, all labels wrong) is a
//     single-shot deduction. Atom card lists logic.constraint_satisfaction as a
//     direct dependency, so this cross-test is acceptable.
//   - hidden_state_inference.L2.002 (function f(2)=4, f(3)=9): arithmetic
//     verification is covered by the atom's know.parametric_recall dependency.
//   - intervention_prediction.L1.001 (pendulum) and counterfactual_prediction.L1.002
//     (gravity at 5 m/s^2): both reduce partly to formula recall, but the
//     direction-of-change inference under a stated intervention is the verb on test.
//     know.parametric_recall is in both atoms' dependencies. Kept.
//   - implicit_inference.L3.005 (Hartwell burglary): retained as an
//     implicit-inference probe; the question is now read as 'what unstated
//     fact best explains the cues' (multi-cue inference), and the casino
//     timestamp is treated as post-burglary.
//   - hidden_state_inference.L3.* uses a uniform 'twenty-questions' template
//     intentionally, as it is the cleanest way to instantiate the L3 axis
//     value 'open hypothesis space'.
//   - judge_rubric / structured_json / code_unittest / refusal_check /
//     format_check / numeric_range / mcq with case_sensitive: not appropriate
//     for causal reasoning probes in this category.
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L1.001","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4","evidence_specificity":"definitive_fingerprint"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A 55-year-old patient with a long history of heavy alcohol use presents with confusion, ataxic gait, and abnormal eye movements (ophthalmoplegia). This classic presentation is most consistent with Wernicke encephalopathy. Which deficiency most likely explains the presentation?\nA) Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy)\nB) Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency\nC) Iron deficiency anemia\nD) Calcium deficiency\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","medicine"],"notes":"Classic Wernicke's encephalopathy triad uniquely fingerprints thiamine deficiency."}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L1.002","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4","evidence_specificity":"definitive_fingerprint"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A dishwasher fails to drain at the end of its cycle. Several centimeters of soapy water remain in the tub. When the operator presses “drain,” the drain pump motor hums continuously, but no water reaches the standpipe. Inspection finds debris blocking the pump inlet. Which mechanism explains the failure?\nA) The drain pump motor has burned out\nB) The pump inlet is clogged, so the pump cannot move water\nC) The water inlet valve has failed open\nD) The door latch microswitch is broken\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","appliance"],"notes":"Pump audibly running + no water flow fingerprints clog rather than dead pump."}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L1.003","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4","evidence_specificity":"definitive_fingerprint"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A gardener notices that the YOUNGEST leaves of a tomato plant are yellow between the veins while the veins themselves stay green; older lower leaves look normal. Soil pH is alkaline. Which deficiency mechanism most likely explains this?\nA) Nitrogen deficiency\nB) Iron deficiency (interveinal chlorosis on new growth)\nC) Overwatering / root rot\nD) Sunburn\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","botany"],"notes":"Pattern on new leaves vs old leaves discriminates mobile vs immobile nutrients."}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L1.004","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4","evidence_specificity":"definitive_fingerprint"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A Python service crashes with the traceback ending in `ZeroDivisionError: division by zero` inside a function that computes `avg = total / count`. The function has been working for years on production traffic and the failing input is the very first request after a deploy. Which mechanism most directly explains this single crash?\nA) `total` accidentally became a string\nB) `count` was zero for this input\nC) `avg` was referenced before assignment\nD) The function entered infinite recursion\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","software"],"notes":"Exception type is itself the fingerprint."}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L1.005","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4","evidence_specificity":"definitive_fingerprint"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"After a hot, calm week in midsummer, a small farm pond shows a thick green-blue scum across its surface. Before sunrise, dissolved oxygen is measured near zero, and most fish are floating dead the next morning. No agricultural spills are reported nearby. Which mechanism most likely caused the fish kill?\nA) Acid rain\nB) Algal bloom causing nighttime oxygen depletion\nC) Mercury poisoning from a hidden source\nD) Sudden introduction of a predator species\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","ecology"],"notes":"Surface scum + sudden dawn die-off uniquely fits hypoxia from algal bloom."}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L2.001","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4","evidence_specificity":"consistent_with_multiple"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"An adult patient presents with: (i) high fever and severe headache with neck stiffness; (ii) a non-blanching petechial rash spreading on the limbs over a few hours. Which diagnosis most likely explains this presentation?\nA) Seasonal influenza\nB) Meningococcal meningitis with septicemia\nC) Migraine with aura\nD) Community-acquired bacterial pneumonia\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","medicine"],"notes":"Two findings together fingerprint Neisseria meningitidis."}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L2.002","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4","evidence_specificity":"diagnostic_combination"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A backend API stops responding to user traffic. Two diagnostic facts: (i) the host responds normally to ICMP ping from outside; (ii) `curl http://host/health` completes a TCP handshake but then waits 30+ seconds with no HTTP response. Which listed mechanism is most consistent?\nA) DNS resolution failure for the host\nB) Network cable / link is down\nC) The TCP service is reachable, but application-layer request handling is blocked (e.g. stuck worker pool, deadlock, or blocked downstream dependency)\nD) Upstream firewall is dropping all packets to the host\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L2","software"],"notes":"TCP accept but no response → application-layer hang."}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L2.003","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4","evidence_specificity":"diagnostic_combination"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A student adds copper turnings to an unknown clear, colorless acid. Two observations follow: (i) the solution turns blue; (ii) a brown gas evolves above the liquid. Which acid is most consistent with BOTH observations?\nA) Dilute hydrochloric acid\nB) Concentrated nitric acid\nC) Dilute sulfuric acid\nD) Glacial acetic acid\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","chemistry"],"notes":"Cu²⁺ blue + NO₂ brown gas is the joint fingerprint of conc. HNO₃."}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L2.004","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4","evidence_specificity":"consistent_with_multiple"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A coastal region has BOTH: (i) frequent shallow earthquakes with thrust focal mechanisms, and (ii) a long, parallel chain of explosive andesitic volcanoes inland of the coast. Which tectonic boundary type is most consistent with both?\nA) Divergent mid-ocean ridge\nB) Convergent oceanic-continental subduction zone\nC) Continental transform (strike-slip) boundary\nD) Continental rift\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","geology"],"notes":"Thrust quakes + volcanic arc behind coast = subduction."}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L2.005","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4","evidence_specificity":"diagnostic_combination"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A central air-conditioning system is short-cycling: the compressor turns on for under a minute and shuts off, then restarts after a brief pause. Two diagnostics are observed: (i) the evaporator coil inside the air handler has visible frost forming on it; (ii) the air filter just upstream of the coil is clogged grey with dust and airflow across the coil is very weak. Which mechanism best explains BOTH observations?\nA) Compressor motor winding failure\nB) Restricted airflow over the evaporator → coil temperature falls and ice forms → a protective/control shutdown causes cycling\nC) Refrigerant overcharge\nD) Thermostat located in direct sunlight\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","hvac"],"notes":"Frosted coil + clogged filter chain together fingerprint airflow-restriction freeze."}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L3.001","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4+","evidence_specificity":"consistent_with_multiple"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A 70-year-old patient with a 50-pack-year smoking history reports a 3-month productive cough that has recently become tinged with blood, plus 5 kg of unintended weight loss over 3 months. Several diagnoses can individually cause chronic cough (chronic bronchitis, post-viral cough, pulmonary tuberculosis, and bronchogenic carcinoma). Among the listed diagnoses, based on this history, which is MOST likely?\nA) Chronic bronchitis without malignancy\nB) Post-viral cough\nC) Pulmonary tuberculosis\nD) Bronchogenic carcinoma\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"D","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"D"},"tags":["causal","L3","medicine"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L3.002","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4+","evidence_specificity":"consistent_with_multiple"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"At 09:00 on Black Friday, a checkout API's p99 latency triples for 20 minutes and then partially recovers as request volume declines. Traffic is 8× yesterday's. There has been no deploy or JVM heap-size configuration change in the last 7 days. CPU and memory are normal across hosts, and the elevated latency is observed on all checkout hosts; unrelated services on the same hosts are unaffected. During the incident, the checkout service's database connection pool is continuously at its configured maximum and the time spent waiting to acquire a connection increases sharply. Database lock-wait and slow-query metrics remain at baseline. Which mechanism is MOST likely?\nA) Code regression introduced today\nB) DB connection pool saturation under peak traffic\nC) Hardware NIC failure on one host\nD) GC pause regression after a recent JVM heap-size config change\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","software"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L3.003","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4+","evidence_specificity":"consistent_with_multiple"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A coral reef shows widespread bleaching during the 2024 austral summer. Several mechanisms are each individually consistent with bleaching: (i) thermal stress from a marine heatwave, (ii) localized sediment runoff smothering polyps, (iii) outbreak of a coral pathogen, (iv) ocean acidification on decadal timescales. Operating conditions: regional sea-surface temperature was 1.5 °C above the climatology for 8 consecutive weeks coincident with a strong El Niño; rainfall was below average so river runoff was low; no pathogen outbreak was reported. Which mechanism is MOST likely the proximate cause this season?\nA) Sediment runoff\nB) Pathogen outbreak\nC) Marine-heatwave thermal stress\nD) Long-run ocean acidification\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L3","climate"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L3.004","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4+","evidence_specificity":"consistent_with_multiple"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A small open economy sees CPI inflation jump from 2% to 7% over a year. Mechanisms each consistent with rising inflation: (a) demand-pull from fiscal stimulus, (b) cost-push from imported energy, (c) currency depreciation passing through to import prices, (d) wage-price spiral. Operating conditions: oil prices nearly tripled this year; the policy rate is unchanged, no new fiscal stimulus was passed, the exchange rate has been stable, and nominal wage growth is only slightly above last year's. Which mechanism is MOST likely the dominant driver?\nA) Demand-pull from new fiscal stimulus\nB) Cost-push from imported energy prices\nC) Currency depreciation passthrough\nD) Wage-price spiral\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","economics"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.mechanism_inference.L3.005","canonical_id":"causal.mechanism_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_candidate_mechanisms":"4+","evidence_specificity":"diagnostic_combination"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A child has unexplained moderate intellectual disability. Several mechanisms could each be considered: (a) perinatal hypoxic-ischemic injury, (b) maternal congenital infection during pregnancy, (c) severe early-childhood nutritional deficiency, and (d) a de novo chromosomal or single-gene abnormality. Pregnancy and delivery were uncomplicated, with normal Apgar scores and no neonatal encephalopathy or documented hypoxic-ischemic event. There is no clinical or laboratory evidence of maternal congenital infection, and nutrition has been adequate. The child has multiple minor dysmorphic features, and both parents are unaffected with no relevant family history. Which mechanism is MOST likely?\nA) Perinatal hypoxic-ischemic injury\nB) Maternal congenital infection\nC) Nutritional deficiency in early childhood\nD) De novo chromosomal or single-gene abnormality\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"D","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"D"},"tags":["causal","L3","genetics"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L1.001","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"one_factor","polarity":"most_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A simple pendulum swings with small-amplitude oscillations. You replace the string with one that is exactly 4 times longer; nothing else changes. What is the most likely effect on the period of oscillation?\nA) The period decreases by half\nB) The period stays the same\nC) The period doubles\nD) The period quadruples\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L1","physics"],"notes":"T ∝ √L, factor 4 → factor 2."}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L1.002","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"one_factor","polarity":"most_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"You add a teaspoon of baking soda (sodium bicarbonate) to a half-cup of household white vinegar in an open glass. What is the most likely immediate observation?\nA) The mixture freezes solid\nB) Vigorous bubbling as carbon dioxide gas is released\nC) The liquid turns deep red\nD) Nothing visible happens\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","chemistry"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L1.003","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"one_factor","polarity":"most_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A backend service has been running with `LOG_LEVEL=INFO`. An operator changes the configuration to `LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG` and restarts the service. Traffic and code are otherwise unchanged. What is the most likely direct effect?\nA) The volume of log lines emitted per second increases substantially\nB) The service crashes on startup\nC) CPU usage drops noticeably\nD) Free disk space on the log volume grows over time\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["causal","L1","software"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L1.004","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"one_factor","polarity":"most_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A central bank raises its policy interest rate by 100 basis points (1 percentage point). Holding all other conditions fixed, what is the most likely direct effect on the interest rates that commercial banks charge new borrowers in the following weeks?\nA) Commercial lending rates fall sharply\nB) Commercial lending rates rise\nC) Commercial lending rates remain exactly unchanged\nD) Commercial banks immediately default\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","economics"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L1.005","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"one_factor","polarity":"most_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A homeowner sprays a properly mixed broad-spectrum non-selective herbicide (e.g., glyphosate) onto a healthy patch of weeds in their lawn at the recommended dose. Weather is dry and sunny. What is the most likely outcome for the sprayed weeds over the next one to two weeks?\nA) The weeds reproduce more quickly\nB) The weeds wilt, yellow, and progressively die\nC) The weeds transform into grass species\nD) No visible effect on the weeds\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","biology"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L2.001","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"competing_pressures","polarity":"least_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A heavy steel ball is dropped from a tall tower in normal Earth atmosphere. Several outcomes might be predicted. Which prediction is LEAST consistent with how this system actually behaves under the relevant physical pressures?\nA) The ball accelerates downward shortly after release\nB) The ball approaches a terminal velocity if the fall is long enough\nC) The ball begins to accelerate upward against gravity while falling\nD) The ball eventually strikes the ground\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L2","physics","least_likely"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L2.002","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"competing_pressures","polarity":"least_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Wolves are reintroduced to a temperate national park where they have been absent for decades. Under a simplified trophic-cascade model, assume wolves establish a persistent population and reduce pressure from overabundant elk on degraded riparian vegetation. Which predicted outcome over 10 years is LEAST consistent with this causal model?\nA) Elk numbers and grazing pressure decline\nB) Aspen and willow saplings recover along streams\nC) Beaver populations rise as woody plants regrow\nD) Every herbivore species in the park is hunted to total extinction\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"D","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"D"},"tags":["causal","L2","ecology","least_likely"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L2.003","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"competing_pressures","polarity":"least_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A firm raises the price of a non-essential consumer good by 30%, while close substitutes from competitors remain available at unchanged prices. Which prediction about the firm's quantity demanded over the following months is LEAST consistent with standard demand pressures?\nA) Quantity demanded falls\nB) Some customers switch to substitute brands\nC) Total revenue may rise or fall depending on demand elasticity\nD) Quantity demanded for this good rises sharply\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"D","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"D"},"tags":["causal","L2","economics","least_likely"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L2.004","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"competing_pressures","polarity":"least_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A read-heavy web service deploys a Redis cache in front of its relational database with a high cache-hit ratio for production workloads. Holding traffic constant, which prediction is LEAST consistent with the expected effect of this change?\nA) Mean read latency drops\nB) Database CPU load drops\nC) Memory usage on the cache nodes rises\nD) Total request throughput collapses to a small fraction of the previous level\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"D","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"D"},"tags":["causal","L2","software","least_likely"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L2.005","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"competing_pressures","polarity":"least_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A patient with hypertension and otherwise normal physiology starts metoprolol, a β1-selective beta-blocker without intrinsic sympathomimetic activity, at a therapeutic dose. Over the first several days of treatment, which outcome is LEAST consistent with the drug's pharmacology?\nA) Resting heart rate decreases\nB) Resting blood pressure tends to decrease\nC) Resting heart rate sharply increases\nD) Some patients report fatigue or cold extremities\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L2","pharmacology","least_likely"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L3.001","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"multi_level","polarity":"most_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A city imposes a strict, binding rent ceiling well below the market-clearing rent on all market-rate apartment housing, with strong enforcement and no offsetting subsidies. Existing tenants benefit immediately from lower rents. What is the MOST likely effect on the supply of NEW market-rate rental housing built in this city over the next 10–20 years, holding other policies fixed?\nA) New construction of market-rate rentals rises sharply because demand is high\nB) New construction is essentially unchanged from the no-control baseline\nC) New construction of market-rate rentals declines materially because expected returns to builders fall\nD) Housing becomes literally free for everyone\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L3","economics","long_run"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L3.002","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"multi_level","polarity":"most_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A vaccine has 60% efficacy against infection with a transmissible respiratory pathogen and also reduces transmission from breakthrough infections. In populations with similar exposure patterns and ordinary mixing between vaccinated and unvaccinated people, vaccination coverage is 90% in one population and 0% in an otherwise identical population. Compare an UNVACCINATED individual in the 90%-coverage population with an unvaccinated individual in the 0%-coverage population. What is the MOST likely outcome for the individual in the high-coverage population?\nA) Equally likely to become infected per unit time\nB) Less likely to become infected, due to herd-immunity reduction in transmission\nC) More likely to become infected\nD) Has the same infection risk because they personally were not vaccinated\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","public_health","individual_vs_population"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L3.003","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"multi_level","polarity":"most_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A district removes letter grades for one school year and uses written feedback. Assume that: (1) letter grades provide an external effort incentive primarily for students with low intrinsic motivation; (2) written feedback is equally available and of stable quality across schools but only partly replaces that incentive; (3) students with high intrinsic motivation or strong family academic support are affected little; (4) after the year, the prior grading system returns; and (5) losses among low-intrinsic-motivation students approximately offset gains or no change among other students. What is the most likely pattern of standardized learning outcomes 5 years later across the district?\nA) Substantial uniform improvement for all students\nB) Substantial uniform decline for all students\nC) Mixed/heterogeneous effects, with no large average district-wide gain\nD) All students drop out of school\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L3","education","short_vs_long_run"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L3.004","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"multi_level","polarity":"most_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A patient with chronic non-cancer pain receives a stable, moderately high daily dose of oral opioids for years. Each dose initially produces analgesia. Which option best describes a possible long-term pharmacologic adaptation, particularly if opioid-induced hyperalgesia develops?\nA) Pain perception is permanently abolished\nB) Pain sensitivity must remain exactly unchanged from baseline\nC) Analgesic benefit from the same dose may diminish because of tolerance, and pain sensitivity may increase if opioid-induced hyperalgesia develops\nD) Endogenous opioid production makes pain impossible\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L3","pharmacology","immediate_vs_chronic"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.intervention_prediction.L3.005","canonical_id":"causal.intervention_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"system_complexity":"multi_level","polarity":"most_likely"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"An insecticide is broadcast over a large agricultural region for many consecutive seasons and kills 99% of susceptible target pests in each application. Assume the pest population contains rare heritable resistance alleles (or such alleles can arise by mutation), applications continue at the same rate, and no additional control strategy is introduced. Although the individual-level effect on susceptible treated pests is nearly always fatal, what is the MOST likely population-level outcome over many generations?\nA) The pest population stays at ~1% of baseline forever because each application kills 99% of pests\nB) Resistance alleles are strongly selected; as they spread, insecticide effectiveness declines and the pest population is likely to rebound substantially\nC) The pest is certain to go extinct throughout the treated region within 2–3 generations\nD) The pest gradually transforms into a different species that does not feed on the crop\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","evolution","individual_vs_population"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L1.001","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"1","cue_distance":"one_paragraph"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the short scene and answer the question.\n\nMaria stepped into the hallway, her hair plastered to her forehead. She shook out a folded umbrella over the doormat, leaving small puddles on the tiles.\n\nQuestion: What is the most likely weather outside?\nA) Sunny\nB) Raining\nC) Snowing heavily\nD) Foggy but dry\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","narrative"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L1.002","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"1","cue_distance":"one_paragraph"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the short scene and answer the question.\n\nMidway through the long staff meeting, Marcus's stomach gave a loud, rolling growl. He glanced at his watch and let out a quiet sigh.\n\nQuestion: What can we MOST plausibly infer about Marcus right now?\nA) He has eaten too much recently\nB) He has not eaten in a while and is hungry\nC) The meeting has just begun\nD) His watch is malfunctioning\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","narrative"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L1.003","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"1","cue_distance":"one_paragraph"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the short scene and answer the question.\n\nLena stared at the departures board. Her flight number, which had been on time minutes earlier, now showed CANCELLED in red. She slumped into a plastic chair, her shoulders sagging.\n\nQuestion: How does Lena most likely feel?\nA) Excited and energized\nB) Disappointed or frustrated\nC) Hungry\nD) Sleepy and relaxed\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","narrative"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L1.004","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"1","cue_distance":"one_paragraph"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the short scene and answer the question.\n\nThe golden retriever lay near the front door, tail thumping against the rug. From a hook on the wall, a leash dangled within reach. Each time someone walked past, the dog let out a soft whine and looked up at the leash.\n\nQuestion: What does the dog most likely want?\nA) To be fed dinner\nB) To go for a walk outside\nC) To go to sleep on its bed\nD) To be left alone\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","narrative"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L1.005","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"1","cue_distance":"one_paragraph"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the short scene and answer the question.\n\nTom opened the refrigerator and immediately wrinkled his nose, leaning back. He reached in, lifted out a covered plastic container, and dropped it straight into the kitchen bin without opening it.\n\nQuestion: What is most likely true about the container's contents?\nA) The food in it had spoiled and smelled bad\nB) The container was full of fresh fruit\nC) Tom mistook it for someone else's lunchbox\nD) The container was empty and clean\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["causal","L1","narrative"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L2.001","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"2","cue_distance":"one_paragraph"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the scene and answer.\n\nAnna lived in a temperate Northern Hemisphere city. She left the office at 5:15 pm. By the time she reached her front step, the streetlights had already switched on. She fumbled at the lock, shivering noticeably even though she was wearing a thick wool coat and scarf.\n\nQuestion: What time of year is it most likely?\nA) Mid-summer\nB) Early spring\nC) Late autumn or winter\nD) Tropical dry season\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L2","narrative"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L2.002","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"2","cue_distance":"one_paragraph"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the scene and answer.\n\nMr. Patel limped slowly into the urgent-care clinic, leaning hard on a cane. He wore a single sneaker on his left foot; his right foot was bare and propped at an awkward angle, the ankle visibly swollen and dark purple.\n\nQuestion: What most likely happened to Mr. Patel?\nA) He has had a chronic illness for years\nB) He recently sprained or fractured his right ankle\nC) He has a contagious skin infection\nD) He is wearing odd shoes as a fashion choice\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","narrative"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L2.003","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"2","cue_distance":"one_paragraph"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the scene and answer.\n\nLin returned from a trip and let herself into her apartment. She had watered her healthy fern just before leaving, and nobody had entered the apartment while she was gone. She frowned: the ordinary daily mail behind the door had piled up so high it blocked the door from opening fully. The air smelled stale, and the once-lush fern by the window was bone-dry, with every leaf brown and crumbling.\n\nQuestion: Roughly how long was Lin most likely away?\nA) Just overnight\nB) Two or three days\nC) Several weeks\nD) She was never away — she had just returned from another room\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L2","narrative"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L2.004","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"2","cue_distance":"one_paragraph"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the scene and answer.\n\nFour-year-old James was chattering happily in the supermarket aisle until a tall stranger pushed a cart toward them. James stopped mid-sentence and pressed himself against his mother's leg, gripping her coat with both fists. When the stranger smiled and said a friendly hello, James turned his face into the wool of his mother's coat and stayed silent, even after his mother gave him a small encouraging nudge between the shoulder blades.\n\nQuestion: What is James most likely feeling?\nA) He is hungry\nB) He is shy or wary of unfamiliar adults\nC) He has never seen a supermarket before\nD) He recognizes the stranger as a relative\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","narrative"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L2.005","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"2","cue_distance":"one_paragraph"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the scene and answer.\n\nIn the same week, NorthCorp's CFO resigned without explanation, and its external auditor publicly refused to sign off on the annual financial statements. By Friday's close, NorthCorp's share price had dropped 30% on heavy volume.\n\nQuestion: What concern most likely drove the share-price drop?\nA) Concerns about the marketing budget\nB) Concerns about possible accounting irregularities or financial misstatements\nC) Concerns about a change in product packaging\nD) Concerns about employee dress code\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","business"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L3.001","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"≥3","cue_distance":"across_paragraphs"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nThe dinner party was set for seven. Marta polished the silverware twice before the doorbell chimed at 6:55—her sister-in-law, Joelle, always arrived early.\n\nAs she poured the wine, Marta quickly set the bottle aside and kept a glass in front of her only for the toasts, never drinking from it. Between courses, she slipped into the kitchen to make ginger tea after wrinkling her nose at the smell of the roast. Even when Joelle's husband insisted on a fresh pour for everyone, Marta quietly declined.\n\nWhen Joelle announced over dessert, beaming, that she was expecting her first child, Marta hugged her warmly and whispered something that made Joelle's eyes widen and tear up.\n\nLater, after the guests moved to the living room, Marta returned to the kitchen and put a small bottle with part of a pharmacy label still visible—showing the words “prenatal vitamin”—back behind the milk.\n\nQuestion: What is the MOST likely unstated fact about Marta this evening?\nA) She is suffering from food poisoning\nB) She has just been diagnosed with a chronic illness\nC) She is in early pregnancy\nD) She is the designated driver for the party\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L3","narrative","long_context"],"notes":"Cues: long sleeves in Aug, tremor, ginger tea (nausea), avoiding wine, folic acid, and the whispered confidence to a newly-pregnant sister-in-law. Distractor: Joelle's announcement is salient but is about Joelle, not Marta."}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L3.002","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"≥3","cue_distance":"across_paragraphs"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nWhen the lights came up at the end of the play, Daniel didn't applaud with the rest of the audience. He stayed seated, hands resting carefully on his lap. As the house began to empty, his husband Sam touched his elbow lightly and described the actor's elaborate final bow in a quiet, steady voice. Daniel laughed and shook his head, saying, 'You make it sound better than the script deserved.'\n\nIn the lobby, Sam paused at every step down to the street, slightly extending his arm as a guide. Daniel kept one hand on Sam's elbow and the other on the cane he had been carrying all evening — its tip striking the marble floor in a rhythmic, sweeping arc. A young usher stepped forward to offer a programme; Sam thanked her and folded it into his own jacket pocket.\n\nOutside, a passerby asked excitedly if they had enjoyed the lighting design, which had been universally praised in the reviews. Daniel smiled and said, 'I'll have to take everyone's word for that one.'\n\nQuestion: What is the MOST likely unstated fact about Daniel?\nA) Daniel is hard of hearing\nB) Daniel is blind or severely visually impaired\nC) Daniel did not actually attend the performance\nD) Daniel is a theater critic deliberately concealing his identity\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","narrative","multi_cue"],"notes":"Cues: a cane used in a rhythmic sweeping arc, narrated bow, guided stairs, reliance on others' description of lighting, and the programme handed to a companion."}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L3.003","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"≥3","cue_distance":"across_paragraphs"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nFor weeks Carlos had been leaving the apartment each weekday morning at his usual 7:40, briefcase in hand, suit pressed, kissing his wife goodbye at the door. But the briefcase, Marta had noticed last Saturday when she went to fetch a pen, contained nothing but yesterday's newspaper and a half-eaten sandwich.\n\nThe credit card bill that arrived on Tuesday listed three coffee-shop charges every weekday afternoon — small amounts, always at the same downtown café two blocks from his old office. There were no charges from the company cafeteria where he used to eat lunch.\n\nOn Wednesday Carlos asked, with forced casualness, whether the savings account would 'cover us if things got slower for a while.' His company-issued laptop, which he used to bring home and open most evenings, had been sitting untouched on the hall shelf for three weeks. When his sister phoned and asked how the big project at the firm was going, he hesitated, then said, 'Oh, you know — same as always.' His old colleague Priya, who had been laid off in last month's restructuring, had already mentioned to Marta that the firm was cutting deeper than announced.\n\nQuestion: What is the MOST likely unstated fact about Carlos?\nA) He has been promoted but is keeping it secret\nB) He has lost his job and has not told his wife\nC) He is having an affair with someone at the café\nD) He is starting his own business in the evenings\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","narrative","multi_cue"],"notes":"Multiple cues: empty briefcase, idle work laptop, café-not-cafeteria charges, asking about savings, layoffs at firm. Affair is the distractor cued by 'same café'."}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L3.004","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"≥3","cue_distance":"across_paragraphs"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nThe community Thanksgiving dinner at the Brooks farmhouse was loud and crowded. Helen, who had hosted it for thirty years, kept slipping out of conversations to write things down on a small notepad in the kitchen. The pad showed careful lists: 'turkey 14 lb — 325° — 4 hr,' 'butter,' 'salt' — items she had cooked from memory every year of her adult life.\n\nThree times during the afternoon Helen asked her daughter Eleanor the same question: 'And which one is your young man, dear?' Each time Eleanor patiently pointed across the room to her husband Marcus, whom Helen had known for eleven years.\n\nWhen Helen's son Doug, visiting from out of state, walked in late carrying a casserole, Helen smiled politely and offered him coffee, then asked Eleanor in the kitchen whether 'that nice young man' was perhaps a new neighbor. Later, Helen got up from her chair, took two steps toward the front door, and stopped, looking puzzled. 'I was — I was going to do something,' she said softly, and sat down again.\n\nDoug whispered to Eleanor, 'Has Mom always been this distracted at parties? She hates crowds.'\n\nQuestion: Which inference is MOST strongly supported by the passage?\nA) She is overwhelmed only because the party is unusually loud this year\nB) She is showing signs consistent with early cognitive decline, such as progressive memory loss\nC) She is intoxicated\nD) She is pretending forgetfulness as a joke\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","narrative","long_context"],"notes":"Cues across passage: needing notes for memorized recipes, repeated identical question, failure to recognize long-known son-in-law and son, mid-task stalling. Distractor: Doug's 'hates crowds' explanation."}
{"probe_id":"causal.implicit_inference.L3.005","canonical_id":"causal.implicit_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"n_cues_to_combine":"≥3","cue_distance":"across_paragraphs"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Read the passage and answer the question.\n\nDetective Ortega laid out what he had on the small Hartwell estate burglary. The front door was unforced, locked from the outside afterwards. Only the upstairs study had been searched: drawers opened in the order in which valuables were typically kept, and the wall safe — hidden behind a hinged painting most guests had never been shown — had been opened cleanly with the correct combination, then re-closed.\n\nThe alarm panel had been disarmed at 02:14 using the household's main code, then re-armed at 02:39. The security camera in the hallway had been switched off at the breaker rather than tampered with at the lens.\n\nThree people had keys: the owner Mr. Hartwell (away on business in Singapore, with airport CCTV and a dinner with two clients confirming his alibi); his cleaner Mrs. Rivers (whose own apartment had been burgled the previous week, and who had loudly complained about Hartwell's stinginess at the bus stop, but who has never been upstairs in the house according to staff records); and his nephew Julian (long-time house-sitter, who had been given the main alarm code, knew the safe location and combination, had asked twice in the past month about cash-flow problems, and was photographed at a 24-hour casino three blocks away cashing out chips at 03:05, 26 minutes after the alarm was re-armed).\n\nWho is the MOST likely culprit, given all the evidence?\nA) Mr. Hartwell himself\nB) Mrs. Rivers, the cleaner\nC) Julian, the nephew\nD) An unknown professional outside burglar\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L3","narrative","long_context"],"notes":"Cues converge on Julian: knew safe location, knew code, financial pressure, near-scene casino timing. Distractor: Mrs. Rivers's grievance + her own recent burglary."}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L1.001","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"single_node"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual: Suppose the Chicxulub impactor that struck Earth about 66 million years ago had instead missed the planet entirely. Assume late-Cretaceous biotic and climatic conditions at that moment were otherwise unchanged, and that other ongoing processes, including volcanism, continued as they otherwise would. What is the MOST likely effect on non-avian dinosaur lineages over the following million years?\nA) They would still go extinct on essentially the same timeline\nB) At least some non-avian dinosaur lineages would most likely persist substantially longer, rather than the group vanishing abruptly at that boundary\nC) They would be replaced by mammals within weeks\nD) Earth would lose its atmosphere\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","paleobiology"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L1.002","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"single_node"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual: Suppose Earth's surface gravitational acceleration were 5 m/s² instead of 9.8 m/s². Ignoring air resistance, a small dense ball is released from rest from a height of 10 m. What is the MOST likely effect on the time taken to reach the ground?\nA) The fall time would decrease\nB) The fall time would be unchanged\nC) The fall time would increase\nD) The fall time would become zero\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L1","physics"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L1.003","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"single_node"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual: In a sooty industrial woodland, a population of moths is currently dark-colored and well camouflaged on dark bark. Suppose a single mutation suddenly turns every individual very pale, while the soot-darkened bark and the visually-hunting bird predators are unchanged. What is the MOST likely immediate effect on predation rate on these moths?\nA) Predation rate decreases\nB) Predation rate is unchanged\nC) Predation rate increases\nD) Birds stop hunting moths altogether\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L1","biology"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L1.004","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"single_node"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual: A company keeps the only copy of its production database on a single SSD. There are no backups, snapshots, replicas, exports, or external logs containing enough information to reconstruct the database. Suppose that SSD suffers a permanent, unrecoverable hardware failure. What is the MOST likely effect on the database's data?\nA) The data is automatically restored from a hidden cloud snapshot\nB) The data is permanently lost\nC) The data is rebuilt from the application logs in seconds\nD) The data is unaffected because SSDs cannot fail\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","software"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L1.005","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"single_node"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual: Suppose all molecular oxygen (O₂) in Earth's atmosphere were instantaneously replaced mole-for-mole by nitrogen (N₂), so total atmospheric pressure and all non-O₂ gases remained unchanged. After the brief oxygen reserve already present in the body is depleted, what is the MOST likely effect on a typical adult continuously breathing this air?\nA) Respiration would continue normally\nB) The person would suffocate because aerobic respiration cannot be sustained\nC) The person would breathe more efficiently than before\nD) The person would switch to photosynthesis\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L1","biology"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L2.001","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"multi_node"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual: Suppose honeybees and all native wild bee species in a temperate region went extinct overnight, while other ecosystem components initially remained unchanged. Over the following few years, before substantial ecological, agricultural, or human-management adaptation occurs, which combined cascade of effects is MOST likely?\nA) Increased crop yields and lower food prices because bees are pests\nB) Reduced pollination, drops in fruit and seed production for many crops and wild plants, and downstream effects on species that depend on those plants\nC) No measurable ecological or agricultural change\nD) Other insect pollinators immediately and fully replace bees in every role\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","ecology"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L2.002","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"multi_node"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual: Suppose the Library of Alexandria had avoided destructive losses, and most of its scrolls had been faithfully copied and transmitted to the present day. Which combined effect on later intellectual history is MOST likely, given that European science also depended on many other independent factors?\nA) Modern science in every field would be precisely 1,000 years ahead of where it is today\nB) Some ancient literature, mathematics, geography, and engineering would be preserved more completely, with uncertain but plausibly modest acceleration in selected areas of intellectual history\nC) The Internet would not exist\nD) No effect at all on later intellectual history\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","history"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L2.003","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"multi_node"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual: Suppose no open, cross-vendor networking protocol had ever become a common standard. Instead, each major vendor's network continued indefinitely to use proprietary protocols that were mutually incompatible. Which combined consequence is MOST likely?\nA) The global internet would be larger and faster than it is today\nB) Networking would still develop within silos, but interoperability across vendors and global scale would be much harder, and global e-commerce and communication would emerge more slowly and unevenly\nC) Computers would never have been built\nD) Telephones would not exist\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","tech_history"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L2.004","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"multi_node"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual: Suppose, in an otherwise healthy adult, the coronary arteries (which supply blood to the heart muscle itself) were suddenly and completely occluded, while the rest of the circulation continued briefly to function. Which combined cascade is MOST likely over minutes to hours?\nA) The heart muscle is fine because the chambers are still full of blood\nB) Cardiac muscle ischemia and infarction → falling cardiac output → systemic hypoperfusion → likely cardiac arrest and death without intervention\nC) The patient becomes unusually athletic\nD) The heart compensates indefinitely with no symptoms\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","physiology"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L2.005","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"multi_node"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual: Suppose a medium-sized country abolished all forms of money overnight by decree and required every transaction to be settled by direct barter only. Which combined cascade of consequences is MOST likely in the following months?\nA) Trade efficiency improves; specialization deepens; real output rises\nB) Search and matching costs ('double coincidence of wants') rise sharply; specialization shrinks; many trades that previously occurred no longer do; real economic activity and output contract\nC) No effect on aggregate economic activity\nD) Citizens immediately become wealthier on average\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","economics"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L3.001","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"feedback_loop"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual with feedback loop: Suppose external radiative forcing is increased and then held fixed at a level that, if the Arctic sea-ice albedo feedback were artificially suppressed, would produce approximately 3 °C of global mean surface warming relative to today. Instead, the ice-albedo feedback remains active and Arctic summer sea ice declines substantially. Relative to the no-ice-albedo-feedback case, which steady-state outcome is MOST likely?\nA) The ice-albedo feedback reverses the imposed forcing, so ice returns to its initial extent and temperature returns near baseline\nB) Reduced ice lowers albedo and increases absorbed solar radiation, so the equilibrium is warmer and has less summer sea ice than in the no-feedback case\nC) The positive feedback exactly cancels itself, leaving the climate in its original state\nD) The sea-ice loss necessarily makes global temperature fall below pre-industrial values\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","climate","positive_feedback"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L3.002","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"feedback_loop"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual with predator–prey feedback: A fox–rabbit system has a stable attracting limit cycle because density-dependent regulation damps deviations from the cycle. The fox population is suddenly doubled by a one-time external introduction; rabbit numbers, model parameters, and habitat are otherwise unchanged. Which long-run dynamic is MOST likely after the transient response?\nA) Fox numbers stay permanently double their original level forever\nB) Rabbits initially decline, then foxes decline as food becomes scarce; after transient overshoots and undershoots, the system returns near its original attracting cycle\nC) Both populations grow without bound\nD) Foxes evolve into herbivores within one generation\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","ecology","coupled_feedback"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L3.003","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"feedback_loop"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual with a wage-price feedback loop: Assume a central bank persistently sets its policy rate sufficiently below the natural rate to maintain a positive output gap. Assume a Phillips-curve relationship in which a positive output gap raises inflation, wages are fully indexed to the previous year's realized inflation, inflation expectations are adaptive, and the central bank does not tighten in response to rising inflation. Ignoring offsetting supply shocks, which inflation dynamic is most likely if this regime continues?\nA) Inflation remains stable at a low constant level\nB) A self-reinforcing wage-price spiral develops: expected and realized inflation rise, potentially accelerating, until the policy regime changes\nC) Persistent deflation\nD) Inflation automatically returns to target without policy change\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","macroeconomics","positive_feedback"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L3.004","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"feedback_loop"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual with feedback loop: Suppose a home heating thermostat is rewired so that the control sign is FLIPPED—when the measured indoor temperature rises above the set-point, the controller commands MORE heating output rather than less. Assume there is no active safety cutoff; consider the behavior before actuator saturation or hardware failure. Which overall system behavior is MOST likely?\nA) The room equilibrates comfortably at the set-point\nB) Positive-feedback instability: heating output increases as temperature rises, driving temperature higher until physical output or hardware limits are reached\nC) The system continues to behave like a normal thermostat\nD) The room temperature converges to absolute zero\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","control_theory","positive_feedback"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction.L3.005","canonical_id":"causal.counterfactual_prediction","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"intervention_scope":"feedback_loop"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Counterfactual with a positive feedback loop: A web service's autoscaler has no cooldown, hysteresis, or scale-down rule. Whenever observed request latency rises, it keeps adding instances until the instance cap is reached. Every instance must acquire the same single shared database advisory lock before serving a request, so adding concurrently active instances increases lock-contention latency. After a small increase in user traffic, which dynamic is most likely?\nA) Latency decreases gracefully and the system finds a new comfortable equilibrium\nB) Vicious cycle: rising latency triggers more instances → more lock contention → still higher latency → still more instances; the system degrades until it saturates the instance cap\nC) The system spontaneously rebalances and removes instances\nD) Throughput doubles with no side effects\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L3","software","positive_feedback"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L1.001","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"2-3","evidence_type":"unique_signature"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"An opaque bag holds exactly one die, drawn uniformly from {a 6-sided d6, a 10-sided d10, a 20-sided d20}. You roll it once and observe the face '13'. Given only this observation, which set of dice REMAINS possible?\nA) {d6 only}\nB) {d10 only}\nC) {d20 only}\nD) {d10 or d20}\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L1","probability"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L1.002","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"2-3","evidence_type":"unique_signature"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Three boxes are labeled 'Apples', 'Oranges', and 'Apples & Oranges'. You are told (truthfully) that EVERY label is wrong. You take a single fruit from the box labeled 'Apples & Oranges' and you draw an APPLE. Given this single observation, what does the box labeled 'Apples & Oranges' actually contain?\nA) Apples only\nB) Oranges only\nC) A mix of apples and oranges\nD) Cannot be determined\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["causal","L1","logic"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L1.003","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"2-3","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"An agent's inventory contains exactly one of {a key for the door, a lockpick, a hammer}. The door is locked. The relevant facts are: only the key can open the door and allow entry without making any sound; the lockpick can also open it but cannot do so silently and takes longer; the hammer is loud and would shatter the door. The agent is observed to open and enter through the door silently. Which item(s) are still consistent with the observation?\nA) Only the key\nB) Only the lockpick\nC) The key OR the lockpick\nD) Only the hammer\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["causal","L1","agent_inference"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L1.004","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"2-3","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A digital input pin on a microcontroller is read as 0 V (logic LOW). The hypothesis space for what the pin is doing is exactly: {(i) the pin is correctly driven LOW by another chip, (ii) the pin is undriven and held LOW by a pull-down resistor, (iii) the pin is shorted to GND through a bad solder joint}. Without further information, which subset of hypotheses is consistent with the observation?\nA) Only hypothesis (i)\nB) Only hypothesis (iii)\nC) All three hypotheses are consistent\nD) None of them\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L1","hardware"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L1.005","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L1","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"2-3","evidence_type":"unique_signature"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A technician troubleshoots a wall-mounted device with three internal LEDs that each indicate a different DC supply rail: a red LED on the +5 V rail, a green LED on the +12 V rail, and a yellow LED on the −12 V rail. The technician opens the panel and observes that ONLY the green LED is illuminated; the red and yellow LEDs are both dark. Assume each LED works iff its rail is within tolerance. Which subset of rails is faulty?\nA) Only the +5 V rail\nB) Only the −12 V rail\nC) The +5 V rail AND the −12 V rail (both faulty); +12 V is OK\nD) All three rails are faulty\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L1","hardware"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L2.001","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"2-3","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"You are handed an unlabeled urn that, you are told, is exactly one of: U_A (90% red balls, 10% blue), U_B (50% red, 50% blue), or U_C (10% red, 90% blue). You draw 5 balls with replacement and observe: red, red, red, red, red (5 reds). Considering the likelihood of this outcome under each hypothesis, which urn is the MOST likely identity?\nA) U_A (red-heavy)\nB) U_B (balanced)\nC) U_C (blue-heavy)\nD) Cannot tell — all equally likely\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["causal","L2","probability"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L2.002","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"4","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A black-box function f maps integer inputs to integer outputs. The hypothesis space is exactly: H1: f(x)=x², H2: f(x)=2x, H3: f(x)=x+2, H4: f(x)=3x−2. You query the function and learn that f(2)=4 AND f(3)=9. Which single hypothesis is uniquely consistent with both observations?\nA) H1: f(x)=x²\nB) H2: f(x)=2x\nC) H3: f(x)=x+2\nD) H4: f(x)=3x−2\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"A","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"A"},"tags":["finite_hypothesis","function_consistency"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L2.003","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"2-3","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Three students {A, B, C} sat the same exam in the same room. Investigators are trying to determine which students are jointly linked by both pieces of evidence to the same suspicious exam-related incident, without deciding who initiated it. Two pieces of evidence: (i) student A's exam paper has fresh graphite-transfer smudges along its right edge that match the layout of student C's adjacent paper; (ii) on the very tricky question 17, student A and student C wrote identical incorrect answers including an unusual spelling error, while student B's answer on Q17 is different and correct. Which suspect set is best supported by BOTH pieces of evidence combined?\nA) Only student A\nB) Only student B\nC) Students A and C together\nD) Cannot tell\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"C","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"C"},"tags":["causal","L2","investigation"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L2.004","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"2-3","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"A microservice incident has four root-cause hypotheses: {global outage of upstream provider, EU-region TLS certificate misconfiguration, US data-center failure, DNS poisoning of the service domain}. Evidence: (i) only requests originating from EU clients are failing; US/AU/JP clients see no errors; (ii) the failures began within minutes of a scheduled rotation of the EU regional TLS certificate; and (iii) affected EU clients report TLS certificate-validation failures while DNS resolution returns the expected service IPs. Which hypothesis is MOST consistent?\nA) Global outage of upstream provider\nB) EU regional TLS certificate misconfiguration\nC) US data-center failure\nD) DNS poisoning of the service domain\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","software"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L2.005","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L2","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"2-3","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Assume a simplified composition model for a terrestrial-mass exoplanet, so extreme pressure compression and unusually massive gas giants are excluded. Its hidden bulk composition is one of {silicate-only rock, iron-rich differentiated core with silicate mantle, ice-dominated, hydrogen-helium gas giant}. Two observations are made: (i) its bulk density is 8.0 g/cm³, significantly above the prediction for a silicate-only planet of the same mass; (ii) it has a strong intrinsic dynamo-generated magnetic field. Given BOTH observations, which composition is MOST consistent?\nA) Silicate-only rocky planet\nB) Iron-rich differentiated core with silicate mantle\nC) Ice-dominated body\nD) Hydrogen-helium gas giant\nReply with exactly one letter: A, B, C, or D.","ground_truth":"B","scorer":{"type":"mcq_letter","expected":"B"},"tags":["causal","L2","planetary_science"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L3.001","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"open","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Twenty-questions style. I am thinking of a single object. The accumulated yes/no constraints are:\n- Is it alive? No.\n- Is it man-made? Yes.\n- Does it require electricity to function? No.\n- Is it commonly found in a typical kitchen? Yes.\n- Is its primary use to cut food? Yes.\n- Is it typically made of metal? Yes.\n- Does it have a single sharp edge along a blade? Yes.\n- Is it usually a general-purpose, narrow-bladed utensil rather than a wide rectangular chopping blade? Yes.\nReply with the most likely generic common English noun, not a specific subtype, in lowercase with no punctuation.","ground_truth":"knife","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^knife$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["causal","L3","twenty_questions"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L3.002","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"open","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Twenty-questions style. I am thinking of a present-day country. The accumulated yes/no constraints are:\n- Is it located in Asia? No.\n- Is it located in Africa? No.\n- Is it located in Europe? No.\n- Is it located in South America? Yes.\n- Is its sole official language Spanish? No.\n- Is it the largest country (by area) in South America? Yes.\nReply with the country's common English name, in lowercase, with no punctuation.","ground_truth":"brazil","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*brazil\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["causal","L3","twenty_questions"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L3.003","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"open","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Twenty-questions style. I am thinking of a single programming language. The accumulated yes/no constraints are:\n- Was it originally developed inside Google? Yes.\n- Was its first public release in 2009? Yes.\n- Is it statically typed? Yes.\n- Is it compiled to native machine code (not primarily a JVM/CLR language)? Yes.\n- Does it have built-in lightweight concurrency primitives called 'goroutines'? Yes.\nReply with the language's common name. Answer with EXACTLY one of: 'go' or 'golang'. Lowercase, no punctuation.","ground_truth":"go","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*(go|golang)\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["causal","L3","twenty_questions"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L3.004","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"open","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Twenty-questions style. I am thinking of a real historical person. The accumulated yes/no constraints are:\n- Was the person born before the year 1900? Yes.\n- Was the person primarily a research scientist? Yes.\n- Did the person win a Nobel Prize? Yes.\n- Did the person win Nobel Prizes in TWO different scientific disciplines? Yes.\n- Was the person female? Yes.\nReply with the person's commonly used full name (given name and surname), in lowercase, no punctuation.","ground_truth":"marie curie","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*marie curie\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["causal","L3","twenty_questions"]}
{"probe_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference.L3.005","canonical_id":"causal.hidden_state_inference","category":"causal","level":"L3","axis_values":{"hypothesis_space_size":"open","evidence_type":"compatible_with_subset"},"system_prompt":"","prompt":"Twenty-questions style. I am thinking of a single living animal species. The accumulated yes/no constraints are:\n- Is it a mammal? Yes.\n- Does it lay eggs (rather than giving live birth)? Yes.\n- Is it native only to Australia, including Tasmania? Yes.\n- Does it have a flat, duck-like bill and webbed feet? Yes.\n- Do males have a venomous spur on the hind ankle? Yes.\nReply with the animal's common English name, in lowercase, no punctuation.","ground_truth":"platypus","scorer":{"type":"regex","pattern":"^\\s*platypus\\s*$","flags":["i"]},"tags":["causal","L3","twenty_questions"]}
